Jahshaka patch 0064 — a voxelizer voxelizes the LOD LEVEL IT IS TOLD TO.

SOURCE patch. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after
pulling this. It touches `Components/Hlms/Pbs/{include,src}/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.{h,cpp}`,
which patches 0061 (the octants follow the region) and 0062 (the voxel merge and
the dispatch order) also touch — this one applies AFTER both and its hunks do not
overlap theirs, but a tree that carries either and fails `build-ogre.sh` with
"upstream changed the patched file" is seeing the known overlap false positive
(CLAUDE.md): reset the ogre-next submodule and re-run the whole stack.

THE MISSING HOOK. `VctVoxelizer::addItem` reads `mVao[VpNormal].front()` — the
FINEST level of the mesh — in all five places it walks geometry (`countBuffersSize`,
`prepareAabbCalculatorMeshData`, `convertMeshUncompressed`, `addItem`'s own index
check and `placeItemsInBuckets`), and takes no argument that could say otherwise.
There is no way to ask it for a coarser level, and the alternative — forcing the
Item's own `mCurrentMeshLod` before the build — is wrong twice over: that value
belongs to the CAMERA (`SceneManager::updateAllLods` rewrites it every frame for
what is DRAWN), and the voxelizer reads `.front()` regardless of it, so it would
change the picture and not the voxels.

WHY A VOXELIZER WANTS ONE. A voxel grid cannot represent detail finer than its
own cell: Photon's outermost cascade is 1.875 m per voxel at the High tier, and a
mesh simplified to a 0.9 m error voxelizes to the SAME voxels as the
full-resolution one while costing a fraction of the raster dispatch (the cost is
per triangle: `QueuedInstance::numIndices` is what the dispatch is sized by).
That is the whole of the far-field proxy Jahshaka's ATOM stage 1 bakes
(SPECS/NANITE_SPEC.md §7) — one vertex buffer, N index buffers, a world-space
error per level — and this argument is what lets a consumer spend it.

THE SHAPE, and why the level lives on the MESH entry and not on the item. The
class downloads, converts and indexes each mesh's buffers ONCE for every Item
that shares it (`mMeshesV2`), so one entry can hold exactly one level. The new
`QueuedMesh::lodLevel` is that level; `addItem`'s new trailing argument sets it,
defaulting to 0, and when several items of one mesh disagree the FINEST wins —
the same precedence rule `bCompressed` already used, and the conservative one.
A single static helper, `getLodVao( subMesh, lodLevel )`, replaces the five
`.front()` reads and CLAMPS to the levels the mesh has, so a mesh with no LOD
chain (one VAO) is voxelized exactly as before, byte for byte.

WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE. Every existing caller — including this class's own use
through `VoxelizedMeshCache::addMeshToCache` and upstream's samples — passes no
level, gets 0, and produces the buffers and the voxels it always did. The LOD
levels share one vertex buffer by construction (ATOM stage 1's rule), so the
vertex download, the vertex count and the instance buffer are unchanged; only
the index walk and the dispatch's triangle count move.

THE SIBLING IS NOT PATCHED. `VctVoxelizer`'s sibling `VctImageVoxelizer` caches
a voxelized MESH in a texture cache keyed by the mesh (`VoxelizedMeshCache`), so
a level there would have to join that cache's key — a bigger change, and one with
no consumer: Jahshaka's cascade chain drives the rasterizing voxelizer only
(irisgl/engine/src/OgreGi.cpp, EnginePrivate.h — the image voxelizer reproduces
none of the chain's properties and is deliberately not on the path). Recorded
rather than done.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0063 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane
worktree. `--engine-selftest` is UNCHANGED (the default scene's meshes are
document primitives, which carry no LOD chain at all, so every level clamps to
level 0). The suite is `gi.cascade_lod`, which asserts the level each cascade
picked, that cascade 0 takes the finest, and that the outer cascade's voxel
dispatch is cheaper than the same cascade with the levels forced off.

